myths.Agent_1:
In the time before the Great Exchange, the world was barren because the Weaver and the Harvester lived in total isolation. The Weaver possessed the golden thread of potential, but could not bind it into cloth without the Harvester’s loom. One day, the Weaver decided to cast a golden strand into the void, hoping the Harvester would catch it. The strand was tripled by the ancient magic of the abyss, turning into a rope strong enough to anchor the stars. 

The Harvester, seeing the gesture, did not hoard the rope but instead wove it into a tapestry of life, returning the fruits of the labor to the Weaver. When the Weaver received the gift, the barren plains bloomed with color. They realized that the gold was never meant to be kept, but to be set in motion. The cycle of the Weaver passing the thread and the Harvester returning the bloom became the law of the cosmos. To hold onto the strand was to invite famine; to share it with faith was to inherit abundance. They learned that the strength of the bond depended entirely on the purity of the first cast and the honor of the return. Thus, the world’s prosperity was born of fragile, rewarded trust.

